(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-inflammatory-lung-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Inflammatory Lung Diseases Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which disorder results from an organic rather than an inorganic occupational or environmental exposure?","choices":["Coal workers' pneumoconiosis","Silicosis","Asbestosis","Bird fancier's lung"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The organic exposures that cause interstitial lung disease include bird fancier's lung, farmer's lung, mushroom worker's lung and hot tub lung. Inhaled organic dusts such as bird excreta and feathers cause hypersensitivity pneumonitis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 65"},{"q":"Besides a current chest radiograph, which screening test should a patient with silicosis have?","choices":["Yearly eye examination","Pleural fluid cytology","Antibodies to the agent","Tuberculin skin test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis increases the risk of tuberculosis, so patients should have a tuberculin skin test and a current chest radiograph. Treatment is otherwise largely supportive: oxygen, physiotherapy, medications, vaccinations and smoking cessation. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 48, 59, 64, 69"},{"q":"Pneumoconiosis is a chronic fibrotic lung disease caused by inhaling which material?","choices":["Toxic gases such as chlorine","Medications such as amiodarone","Coal or other inorganic dusts","Organic dusts such as moldy hay"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pneumoconiosis is a chronic fibrotic lung disease caused by the inhalation of coal dust or various inorganic or silicate dusts. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis, asbestosis and silicosis all come from these inorganic exposures. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 22, 50, 65, 72"},{"q":"Which part of the lung does interstitial lung disease involve?","choices":["The pleural lining","The large conducting airways","The pulmonary arteries","The gas-exchanging parenchyma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease involves the parenchyma, the portion of the lung involved in gas transfer: the alveoli, alveolar ducts and respiratory bronchioles. It is characterized by alveolar septal thickening, fibroblast proliferation, collagen deposition and, if prolonged, pulmonary fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In which decades of life does sarcoidosis usually begin?","choices":["First and second","Third and fourth","Fifth and sixth","Seventh and eighth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sarcoidosis has its onset in the third and fourth decades of life. It more frequently affects women and has a higher occurrence in North American Black and Northern European white populations; its etiology is unknown. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 40"},{"q":"Which is the most common subtype of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","Acute interstitial pneumonia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is the most common subtype of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia, which is itself the most common diagnosis among patients with interstitial lung disease. Treatment plan and prognosis depend on the subtype diagnosed. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 32, 35, 38"},{"q":"Which laboratory result is elevated in 40 to 80% of patients with sarcoidosis?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody titer","Cytoplasmic antineutrophil antibody","Basement membrane antibodies","Angiotensin-converting enzyme"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin-converting enzyme levels are elevated in 40 to 80% of patients with sarcoidosis. Other laboratory findings include leukopenia, eosinophilia, an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, hypercalcemia in about 5% of patients and hypercalciuria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44"},{"q":"Which occupation exposes workers to free crystalline silica dust?","choices":["Bird breeding","Cheese making","Sandblasting","Demolition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis follows exposure to free crystalline silica dust (silicon dioxide) in mining, sandblasting, quarry work and stone work. The silica deposits in the alveoli, where macrophage invasion causes chronic inflammation, fibrosis and nodular lesions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 60, 65"},{"q":"When may lung transplant be indicated in coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":["When pleural plaques appear","In severe cases","In simple disease","At the time of diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis has no effective curative treatment, so care is supportive with oxygen, vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, and lung transplant may be indicated in severe cases. Complicated disease can progress to progressive massive fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 51, 54, 58"},{"q":"What is the outlook for a patient with mesothelioma?","choices":["Incurable, with poor survival","Curable by early resection","Recovery over 6 to 12 months","Controlled by corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mesothelioma is incurable with poor long-term survival; no treatment is known to prolong survival, and care is primarily supportive. It is nearly always caused by asbestos exposure, and the diagnosis can come from pleural fluid cytology. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 59, 74"},{"q":"Which statement fits the definition of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["A cancer of the lung tissue","Neither malignant nor infectious","A single disorder with one cause","An infection of the alveoli"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease is a broad category of more than 200 disorders characterized by scarring (fibrosis) and/or inflammation of the lung parenchyma. By definition it is not malignant and not caused by an infectious etiology. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 56, 59"},{"q":"About how long after asbestos exposure do patients with asbestosis usually seek medical attention?","choices":["1 year","5 years","15 years","30 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The asbestos history spans 10 to 20 years, and patients usually seek medical attention about 15 years after exposure. Imaging is often normal early in the disease, so the long gap and the occupational history point to the diagnosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Into which two groups are interstitial lung diseases divided by their tissue response?","choices":["Malignant and infectious","Acute and chronic","Fibrotic and granulomatous","Obstructive and restrictive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung diseases fall into two groups by tissue response: inflammation with fibrosis, or granulomatous reactions. Each group can be further divided into known and unknown causes, and most of these diseases have acute and chronic stages. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 11, 26"},{"q":"Below what ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen is the oxygenation criterion for acute respiratory distress syndrome met?","choices":["100 mm Hg","200 mm Hg","300 mm Hg","400 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oxygenation impairment threshold is a ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen under 300 mm Hg (or a ratio of arterial oxygen saturation to fraction of inspired oxygen under 315). Within it, 200 to 300 is mild, 100 to 200 moderate and under 100 severe. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which computed tomography finding characterizes idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia?","choices":["Patchy pleural-based honeycombing","Eggshell hilar node calcification","Pleural plaques at the bases","Upper-lobe nodular opacities"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography in idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia shows diffuse patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing, while the chest radiograph shows progressive fibrosis over several areas. On high-resolution scanning in a patient over 65, it is one of the four no-biopsy criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 53, 58, 60, 63"},{"q":"Amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate and radiation fall under which class of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Unknown cause, granulomatous","Known cause, treatment related","Known cause, occupational","Unknown cause, idiopathic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the classification of interstitial lung disease, the exposure branch of known causes holds a treatment-related box listing radiation, methotrexate, amiodarone, nitrofurantoin and chemotherapeutics. Chemotherapeutic agents, amiodarone and nitrofurantoin also cause drug-induced pulmonary toxicity. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 72"},{"q":"Which occupation carries a risk of asbestos exposure?","choices":["Quarry work","Cheese making","Shipyard work","Sandblasting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asbestos fiber exposure comes from insulation, demolition, shipyard and construction work, with a history spanning 10 to 20 years, and patients usually seek medical attention about 15 years after exposure. The work history is the key clue. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 55, 60, 65"},{"q":"Which interstitial lung disease can produce an episodic presentation?","choices":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Coal workers' pneumoconiosis","Drug-induced lung disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An episodic presentation is less common in interstitial lung disease and can be seen with hypersensitivity pneumonitis, where inhaled organic material leads to illness after exposure, and symptoms can be reversible if the problem is caught early. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 65"},{"q":"In the laboratory workup of interstitial lung disease, what does an increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate point to?","choices":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Active sarcoidosis","Collagen vascular disease","Goodpasture syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among the laboratory clues in interstitial lung disease, an increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate points to active sarcoidosis, which can also show eosinophilia and, in some patients, an elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme level and hypercalcemia. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44"},{"q":"Which evaluation belongs to the yearly follow-up of patients with sarcoidosis?","choices":["Tuberculin skin test","Pleural fluid cytology","Serum antibodies to the agent","Ophthalmologic examination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with sarcoidosis require a yearly physical examination, pulmonary function tests, chest radiograph, electrocardiogram, chemistry panel and an ophthalmologic evaluation to identify and manage ocular inflammation. Iritis and uveitis are indications for corticosteroids. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 48, 59, 64, 69"},{"q":"Which cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome is a direct pulmonary insult?","choices":["Sepsis","Severe pancreatitis","Multiple blood transfusions","Aspiration of gastric contents"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Direct pulmonary causes include aspiration of gastric contents, diffuse pneumonia, lung contusion, toxic gas inhalation, near-drowning, and fat, air, amniotic fluid or thrombus emboli. Sepsis, pancreatitis, shock, trauma outside the chest and blood transfusions are systemic causes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"When may corticosteroids be helpful in hypersensitivity pneumonitis?","choices":["Before any exposure history","In severe cases","In mild acute episodes","In place of avoiding the agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In hypersensitivity pneumonitis, corticosteroids may be helpful in severe cases, along with other immunosuppressant medications, after the responsible agent has been identified and is being avoided. Supportive treatment includes oxygen, bronchodilators and pain medications. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 71"},{"q":"Which disorder is grouped with Goodpasture syndrome among the pulmonary hemorrhage (alveolar filling) syndromes?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis","Systemic sclerosis","Sarcoidosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary hemorrhage syndromes, or alveolar filling disorders, with an inflammatory and fibrotic response, are Goodpasture syndrome, idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis and isolated pulmonary capillaritis. Goodpasture syndrome is marked by basement membrane antibodies. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 25, 32, 34"},{"q":"In the workup of interstitial lung disease, what does a high antinuclear antibody level point to?","choices":["Collagen vascular disease","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Active sarcoidosis","Eosinophilic pneumonia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Among the laboratory findings in interstitial lung disease, a high antinuclear antibody level points to the collagen vascular diseases, the autoimmune connective tissue group that includes lupus, rheumatoid arthritis and systemic sclerosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 44"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph stage of sarcoidosis shows hilar adenopathy together with parenchymal involvement?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage II sarcoidosis shows hilar adenopathy with parenchymal involvement. Stage I is bilateral hilar adenopathy alone, stage III is parenchymal involvement alone with diffuse reticular infiltrates, and stage IV is advanced fibrosis with honeycombing. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which core diagnostic criterion separates acute respiratory distress syndrome from cardiogenic pulmonary edema?","choices":["Opacities explained by effusion","Onset over weeks to months","Unilateral new opacities","Not fully explained by heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A core criterion is noncardiogenic origin: respiratory failure not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload. Cardiogenic pulmonary edema, the main differential, shows cardiomegaly, vascular engorgement and batwing perihilar opacities, and echocardiography helps exclude it. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the most common presenting symptom of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Shortness of breath","Productive cough","Hemoptysis","Chest pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis makes the lung tissue stiff and restrictive, no longer expanding as it once did, which makes breathing harder; patients most commonly present with shortness of breath, typically progressive exertional dyspnea, sometimes with a persistent nonproductive cough. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 23"},{"q":"What role does extracorporeal membrane oxygenation play in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Routine use at diagnosis","Replacement for prone positioning","Salvage for severe refractory cases","First-line therapy for mild cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation is a salvage option reserved for severe, refractory acute respiratory distress syndrome, after the precipitant is treated and low tidal volume ventilation and early proning in moderate to severe disease have been used. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"At what hemoglobin level is transfusion indicated in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["5 g/dL","7 g/dL","9 g/dL","10 g/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome are transfused at a hemoglobin of 7 g/dL; higher hemoglobin targets do not improve patient outcomes. Supranormal, inotrope-driven oxygen delivery is avoided because it can cause harm. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"In which patients can idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis be diagnosed with 90% confidence without surgical lung biopsy?","choices":["Under 40 with a smoking history","Any age with honeycombing","Over 65 with four classic features","Over 65 with crackles alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In patients over 65 years of age, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis can be diagnosed with 90% confidence when there is idiopathic disease with inspiratory crackles, restrictive physiology, radiographic progressive fibrosis over several years, and diffuse patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing on high-resolution computed tomography. Such patients do not need surgical lung biopsy. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 35"}]};
